%%----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

To get and manipulate private properties of an object, you can define a derived class for that object and then define a static method in 
the derived class to get those properties:
1. plannerAStarGrid has access-private properties that cannot be retrieved from the outside
2. Define AStarPlannerInfo class as a derived class to get its properties:


% Note that since AStarPlannerInfo is derived from plannerAStarGrid, it has access to its properties. This is how you use the following class:
	>> planner = plannerAStarGrid; % parent class
	>> infoCellString = AStarPlannerInfo.InfoString(planner); % using a subclass to access planner properties 

classdef AStarPlannerInfo < plannerAStarGrid
    methods (Access=public)
        function infoCellString = InfoString(obj)
            infoCellString = ...
                {...
                'Grid Size',                    char("(" + string(obj.Map.GridSize).join() + ")");
                'Diagonal Search',              num2str(obj.DiagonalSearch);
                'Grid Cost Function',           char(obj.GCostFcn);
                'Heuristic Cost Function',      char(obj.HCostFcn);
                'Start In Grid',                char("(" + string(obj.StartInGrid).join() + ")");
                'Goal In Grid',                 char("(" + string(obj.GoalInGrid).join() + ")");
                'Number of Path Points',        num2str(obj.NumPathPoints);
                'Path Cost',                    num2str(obj.PathCost);
                'Number of Nodes Explored',     num2str(obj.NumNodesExplored);
                };
        end
    end
end

Ideally, one would prefer to derive a subclass from plannerAStarGrid extends its capabilities (along with accessing properties of the parent object).
But, creating a 'copy constructor' in MATLAB is not straightforward.

